{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cguur6WbWS9A4F3vGBpTS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YSsCwhkV928zvdHrivqubJ","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-2553958348/worktree/.pose/adr/2026-10-14-shared-domain-and-optional-worktree-runtime.md","offset":260,"limit":80},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1095,"cache_read_input_tokens":32837,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1095},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"7cdd8f8c-5659-4d4a-ac51-35c7140cfc83","timestamp":"2026-11-10T20:53:50.404Z","request_id":"req_011Cguur6PfdESy9B5HEgETw","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01YSsCwhkV928zvdHrivqubJ":{"file_path":"/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-2553958348/worktree/.pose/adr/2026-10-14-shared-domain-and-optional-worktree-runtime.md","offset":260,"limit":80}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YSsCwhkV928zvdHrivqubJ","type":"tool_result","content":"260\t  handshake, field by field, and a difference is refused naming it, so that a new CLI\n261\t  that finds an old process after an update is refused by name instead of being\n262\t  given a different endpoint and never learning the old one is there.\n263\t- **Authority.** The runtime of slice 1 hosts no domain operation: it answers\n264\t  handshake, status and stop. It confers no authority, signature or presence, and a\n265\t  client that attaches gains nothing it did not have standalone.\n266\t- **Slicing.** B35 is delivered in three specs, each reviewed on its own:\n267\t  `pose-optional-worktree-runtime` (identity, endpoint, handshake, lifecycle,\n268\t  standalone fallback: R1-R4), `pose-worktree-runtime-resident-cache` (R5, R7: the\n269\t  resident reuse of the B33 views, reconciliation, typed results) and\n270\t  `pose-worktree-runtime-qos-and-measurement` (R6, R8: budgets, short queries\n271\t  protected from heavy checks, measured thresholds). Budgets and thresholds are\n272\t  fixed from a measured synthetic corpus with a declared environment, never from a\n273\t  historical sample.\n274\t\n275\t## Amendment: what the optional worktree runtime delivered, and where it departs (B35, 2026-11-10)\n276\t\n277\tThe three slices are done and each was reviewed independently: `pose-optional-worktree-runtime`\n278\t(identity, protected endpoint, handshake, lifecycle, standalone fallback),\n279\t`pose-worktree-runtime-resident-cache` (hosted read checks, typed results, resident memo) and\n280\t`pose-worktree-runtime-qos-and-measurement` (admission lanes and the measurement), with two engine\n281\tspecs that the measurements called for (`pose-index-input-binding-reads-each-transfer-once`,\n282\t`pose-governance-audit-skips-pure-git-reads`). The statement above that \"the runtime of slice 1\n283\thosts no domain operation\" was true of slice 1 only. What stands now, and what differs from the\n284\tdecisions of this ADR:\n285\t\n286\t- **Hosted operations.** The runtime hosts five read checks (`review-check`, `closeout-check`,\n287\t  `artifact-check`, `surface-check`, `roadmap-check`) through `pose runtime run`, as the user that\n288\t  started it, in its worktree; a hosted request carries the caller's identity and is refused,\n289\t  before any handler runs, when it does not match the runtime's engine, build, protocol, schema\n290\t  or worktree; the caller's environment digest must equal the runtime's. They are not writes, but\n291\t  they may refresh derived files as they do standalone (published atomically); a command that may\n292\t  have run (deadline, lost reply) is never repeated elsewhere unasked, which is how the decision\n293\t  \"an ambiguous committed write requires a receipt before a retry\" is met for these commands.\n294\t  Hosted commands go through the domain's own entry (`mainCommand`), not a new typed interface:\n295\t  the typed-domain extraction of the first bullet of the decision stays with B31.\n296\t- **Caches.** The B33 views are not what the runtime reuses (the measurements showed they do not\n297\t  address the cost). It keeps, between commands, the output of the Git reads that are pure\n298\t  functions of object ids or of the commit HEAD named when they ran, keyed by the commit, the\n299\t  content of the shallow file and of the repository config, with a byte budget and\n300\t  least-recently-used eviction; no watcher exists, so \"watch events are hints\" is met by not\n301\t  using them: freshness comes from the key, and nothing about mtime or a clean tree is taken as\n302\t  proof. The scoped Git batch reader is untouched.\n303\t- **Budgets and QoS.** Admission is by two lanes (`short`, `heavy`) with slots and bounded queues\n304\t  and a bound on the memo; the global concurrency bound is the sum of the slots. **Not delivered:**\n305\t  CPU quota or affinity awareness, and per-command memory or I/O limits; the runtime bounds what\n306\t  it admits and keeps, not what each command consumes. The measurement records latency, the\n307\t  runtime's resident set and CPU time per scenario, including two simultaneous heavy checks, on one\n308\t  environment; it is not a general benchmark.\n309\t- **Platforms.** Unix domain socket on Linux and macOS (credentials of the peer from the\n310\t  operating system); Windows runs every command standalone and `pose runtime status` says so.\n311\t  The named pipe is a follow-up.\n312\t- **Status.** The decision stands as amended for the delivered scope; what is not delivered above\n313\t  is not adopted and needs its own spec before it is claimed.\n314\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"55406ab6-27ae-412d-ad61-3b87d57e71d0","timestamp":"2026-11-10T20:53:50.410Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-2553958348/worktree/.pose/adr/2026-10-14-shared-domain-and-optional-worktree-runtime.md","content":"  handshake, field by field, and a difference is refused naming it, so that a new CLI\n  that finds an old process after an update is refused by name instead of being\n  given a different endpoint and never learning the old one is there.\n- **Authority.** The runtime of slice 1 hosts no domain operation: it answers\n  handshake, status and stop. It confers no authority, signature or presence, and a\n  client that attaches gains nothing it did not have standalone.\n- **Slicing.** B35 is delivered in three specs, each reviewed on its own:\n  `pose-optional-worktree-runtime` (identity, endpoint, handshake, lifecycle,\n  standalone fallback: R1-R4), `pose-worktree-runtime-resident-cache` (R5, R7: the\n  resident reuse of the B33 views, reconciliation, typed results) and\n  `pose-worktree-runtime-qos-and-measurement` (R6, R8: budgets, short queries\n  protected from heavy checks, measured thresholds). Budgets and thresholds are\n  fixed from a measured synthetic corpus with a declared environment, never from a\n  historical sample.\n\n## Amendment: what the optional worktree runtime delivered, and where it departs (B35, 2026-11-10)\n\nThe three slices are done and each was reviewed independently: `pose-optional-worktree-runtime`\n(identity, protected endpoint, handshake, lifecycle, standalone fallback),\n`pose-worktree-runtime-resident-cache` (hosted read checks, typed results, resident memo) and\n`pose-worktree-runtime-qos-and-measurement` (admission lanes and the measurement), with two engine\nspecs that the measurements called for (`pose-index-input-binding-reads-each-transfer-once`,\n`pose-governance-audit-skips-pure-git-reads`). The statement above that \"the runtime of slice 1\nhosts no domain operation\" was true of slice 1 only. What stands now, and what differs from the\ndecisions of this ADR:\n\n- **Hosted operations.** The runtime hosts five read checks (`review-check`, `closeout-check`,\n  `artifact-check`, `surface-check`, `roadmap-check`) through `pose runtime run`, as the user that\n  started it, in its worktree; a hosted request carries the caller's identity and is refused,\n  before any handler runs, when it does not match the runtime's engine, build, protocol, schema\n  or worktree; the caller's environment digest must equal the runtime's. They are not writes, but\n  they may refresh derived files as they do standalone (published atomically); a command that may\n  have run (deadline, lost reply) is never repeated elsewhere unasked, which is how the decision\n  \"an ambiguous committed write requires a receipt before a retry\" is met for these commands.\n  Hosted commands go through the domain's own entry (`mainCommand`), not a new typed interface:\n  the typed-domain extraction of the first bullet of the decision stays with B31.\n- **Caches.** The B33 views are not what the runtime reuses (the measurements showed they do not\n  address the cost). It keeps, between commands, the output of the Git reads that are pure\n  functions of object ids or of the commit HEAD named when they ran, keyed by the commit, the\n  content of the shallow file and of the repository config, with a byte budget and\n  least-recently-used eviction; no watcher exists, so \"watch events are hints\" is met by not\n  using them: freshness comes from the key, and nothing about mtime or a clean tree is taken as\n  proof. The scoped Git batch reader is untouched.\n- **Budgets and QoS.** Admission is by two lanes (`short`, `heavy`) with slots and bounded queues\n  and a bound on the memo; the global concurrency bound is the sum of the slots. **Not delivered:**\n  CPU quota or affinity awareness, and per-command memory or I/O limits; the runtime bounds what\n  it admits and keeps, not what each command consumes. The measurement records latency, the\n  runtime's resident set and CPU time per scenario, including two simultaneous heavy checks, on one\n  environment; it is not a general benchmark.\n- **Platforms.** Unix domain socket on Linux and macOS (credentials of the peer from the\n  operating system); Windows runs every command standalone and `pose runtime status` says so.\n  The named pipe is a follow-up.\n- **Status.** The decision stands as amended for the delivered scope; what is not delivered above\n  is not adopted and needs its own spec before it is claimed.\n","numLines":55,"startLine":260,"totalLines":314}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01YSsCwhkV928zvdHrivqubJ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
